{#-
  The first admin password, as wp core install should store it.

  wp-login.php checks a password the way PHP magic quotes leave it -
  addslashes()ed - but wp core install stores whatever it is given. Slashing it
  here stores what the login form will send, or a password containing ' " or \
  would install fine and then never log in.

  A template rather than a filter in the task: ansible-core reads a backslash
  in a Jinja string literal inside YAML as written, so '\\' there is two
  characters, not one.
-#}
{{ wordpress_admin_password | replace('\\', '\\\\') | replace("'", "\\'") | replace('"', '\\"') }}
